disk - list drives and volumes

Every block device and the volumes found on it. `mount` attaches one;
`unmount` detaches it, flushing first.

A medium with a filesystem written straight onto it, which is what most
sticks and cards are, is mounted as the whole disk rather than as a
partition of one. The mounted column says where each went.

The boot medium carries three volumes: the first holds the system, the
second is mounted at /cfg and keeps the settings, and the third is /home
itself. They are found by the signature in the medium's own partition
table, so a machine with several disks attaches the one it actually
booted from.

A disk on a bus the kernel does not drive itself, such as one on USB,
appears here like any other once its driver offers it. It arrives under
/media on its own and takes its mount with it when it is unplugged, so
a path that stops working is a medium that has gone rather than a fault.
